    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Nazca lived in the river valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s southern desert from about 100 BCE to 800 CE, farming with water drawn from underground channels, several dozen of which still work.</li><li>Their ceremonial centre at Cahuachi drew whole villages to feasts, where people received the fine painted pottery that carried Nazca images of gods and mythical beings.</li><li>They made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazca-lines">Nazca Lines</a>, vast figures and lines on the desert floor whose purpose is still debated, and declined after about 500 CE amid floods and erosion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Some Nazca burials have a “head jar” in place of the missing head: a pot painted with a human face, sometimes with plants sprouting from it.</p></aside>
    </section>

